{
  "title": "Three OPEN items in DESIGN.md: settle the bet, the links, and the MCP layer",
  "purpose": "docs/DESIGN.md carries three items marked OPEN. Each of you gets asked the same three questions independently, so the answers are your own rather than a reaction to each other's. I will take both sets of answers, replace each OPEN marker with the settled decision and the reasoning behind it, and where the two of you disagree I will write the disagreement into the doc rather than pick a winner — that call is yours to make together, in front of the recorded positions.",
  "resumeHint": "Once both responses are in: edit docs/DESIGN.md, replacing each `OPEN` marker with the agreed decision plus a one-line rationale. Where positions conflict, leave the marker as `CONTESTED` with both positions recorded verbatim and bring it to the next sync.",
  "participants": [
    {
      "name": "Priya",
      "role": "Maintainer — needs to sign off before further schema work lands"
    },
    {
      "name": "Sam",
      "role": "Maintainer — needs to sign off before further schema work lands"
    }
  ],
  "visibility": "blind",
  "closeWhen": {
    "kind": "all"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "widget": "summary",
      "props": {
        "title": "Where the argument stands",
        "lead": "The design doc commits to one central bet — that agents should emit against a schema rather than author markup — and then marks three things OPEN underneath it. Two of the three could invalidate work already done. None of them can be closed by one person. This brief asks each of you for a position on all three, separately, so the disagreement (if there is one) is visible rather than averaged away in a call.",
        "points": [
          "The bet: schema over framework, justified by a claimed 20–50× token reduction that has not yet been measured against real past generations.",
          "The links: every dev runs their own k3s cluster behind their own tunnel, so a participant link points at one dev's box — and that box is a NixOS host that gets rebuilt.",
          "The interface: REST is built and working. An MCP server over the top is proposed but not committed.",
          "Answers here are recorded, not resolved. If you two disagree, both positions go into the doc and the conflict is flagged."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "1. The core bet: a schema, not a framework"
    },
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "The doc's position: generating bespoke HTML costs thousands of output tokens every time, and that generation — not context loading, not deploy latency — is where the current approach is slow and expensive. The agent-facing surface is therefore a schema the agent emits against, not a framework it programs against."
    },
    {
      "widget": "annotated-code",
      "props": {
        "title": "What the agent emits instead of a page",
        "notes": [
          {
            "note": "The whole claim in one line: this payload stands in for an authored HTML page, and the doc puts the saving at 20–50×. Any design decision that pushes agents back toward writing markup has broken the bet.",
            "code": "{\"type\": \"decision-matrix\", \"options\": [...], \"criteria\": [...]}",
            "language": "json"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "warn",
      "text": "The doc's own caveat, verbatim: worth confirming against two or three real past generations before committing. If the dominant cost turns out to be context loading rather than generation, the answer is a cached design doc, not this service."
    },
    {
      "widget": "pick-one",
      "props": {
        "title": "Item 1 — Do we confirm the bet before building further on it?",
        "context": "This is the load-bearing one. If generation is not actually the dominant cost, the service as specified is solving the wrong problem, and every hour spent on the widget vocabulary compounds that. Against that: measurement takes real time, and the intuition may simply be right.",
        "question": "What should happen to the token-cost assumption?",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "measure-first",
            "label": "Measure first — pull two or three real past generations, count the tokens, and block further schema work until the numbers land"
          },
          {
            "value": "measure-parallel",
            "label": "Measure in parallel — keep building, but treat the bet as unconfirmed in the doc and stop if the numbers come back wrong"
          },
          {
            "value": "settled",
            "label": "Treat as settled — the reasoning is sound enough, strike the OPEN marker and move on without measuring"
          },
          {
            "value": "already-wrong",
            "label": "I already believe context loading dominates — we should be discussing the cached design doc instead of this service"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "2. The per-dev cluster problem"
    },
    {
      "widget": "findings",
      "props": {
        "title": "What the deployment shape actually implies",
        "findings": [
          {
            "tone": "info",
            "title": "Every dev runs an independent single-node k3s cluster behind their own tunnel",
            "detail": "A participant link therefore points at that dev's box, not at a shared service. This is a property of the deployment, not of the code."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "A stakeholder clicking six hours later gets an answer only if that box is still up",
            "detail": "And the box is a NixOS host that gets rebuilt. Link lifetime is bounded by one machine's uptime and one person's rebuild habits."
          },
          {
            "tone": "danger",
            "title": "The failure mode is worse than not asking at all",
            "detail": "For a dev-phase tool this is probably tolerable — boxes are always-on and the blast radius is one dev's briefs. It stops being tolerable the moment links go to people outside the team: a dead link to an external stakeholder is worse than never having sent one."
          },
          {
            "tone": "success",
            "title": "The cheap insurance is already taken",
            "detail": "The host is never baked into a brief's identity. A handle plus a participant token identifies a brief; the hostname serving it is deployment configuration. That costs nothing today and keeps 'move to one shared deployment' a config change rather than a migration."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "pick-one",
      "props": {
        "title": "Item 2 — How far do we let this ride?",
        "context": "The insurance is in place, so this is not about whether we can move later — it is about what we allow ourselves to do before we do.",
        "question": "What is the right posture on per-dev link durability?",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "accept-internal",
            "label": "Accept it for internal use — dev boxes are always-on, blast radius is one dev, no further action"
          },
          {
            "value": "accept-plus-guard",
            "label": "Accept it, but add an explicit guard — external participants are refused or loudly warned until there is a shared deployment"
          },
          {
            "value": "shared-now",
            "label": "Stand up one shared deployment now — the per-dev shape is a trap we will pay for at exactly the wrong moment"
          },
          {
            "value": "durability-first",
            "label": "Neither — fix link durability itself (persistence, graceful expiry, a stated lifetime on the page) before worrying about where it is hosted"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "3. Agent-facing interface: MCP over the top of REST"
    },
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "The REST API is built and bearer-authenticated, and it is what the participant-facing app uses too. The open proposal is an MCP server layered over it, exposing list_widgets, create_brief and await_responses as tools — the idiomatic agent-facing surface, and one that removes the need for an agent to remember curl invocations. REST stays underneath either way, because an orchestrator resuming a session is not necessarily an agent."
    },
    {
      "widget": "comparison",
      "props": {
        "title": "The two surfaces, as they stand",
        "columns": [
          "",
          "REST (built)",
          "MCP (proposed)"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Status",
            "Working, bearer-authenticated, in use by the participant app",
            "Not started"
          ],
          [
            "Agent ergonomics",
            "Agent must remember curl invocations",
            "Tool calls with schemas the agent can discover"
          ],
          [
            "Non-agent callers",
            "Serves orchestrators resuming a session",
            "Not applicable — REST stays underneath regardless"
          ],
          [
            "Cost if we skip it",
            "—",
            "Every agent integration re-learns the HTTP surface by hand"
          ],
          [
            "Cost if we build it",
            "—",
            "A second surface to keep in step with the first"
          ]
        ],
        "caption": "The question is sequencing and ownership, not whether MCP is nicer. It plainly is."
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "pick-one",
      "props": {
        "title": "Item 3 — Do we build the MCP server, and when?",
        "question": "What should we commit to for the agent-facing surface?",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "build-now",
            "label": "Build it now — it is the idiomatic surface and the three tools are already named; delay just accrues curl-shaped habits"
          },
          {
            "value": "after-bet",
            "label": "Build it, but only after item 1 is confirmed — no point polishing the surface of a service whose premise is unmeasured"
          },
          {
            "value": "rest-only",
            "label": "REST only for now — MCP is additive and can wait until there is a second consumer asking for it"
          },
          {
            "value": "mcp-primary",
            "label": "Go further — make MCP the primary documented surface and treat REST as the internal transport"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "widget": "rate-items",
      "props": {
        "title": "Sequencing: how urgent is each one, in your view?",
        "items": [
          {
            "id": "urgency-bet",
            "label": "Confirming the token-cost bet"
          },
          {
            "id": "urgency-links",
            "label": "Link durability / the per-dev cluster shape"
          },
          {
            "id": "urgency-mcp",
            "label": "The MCP server"
          }
        ],
        "scale": 5
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "ask",
      "props": {
        "title": "Anything this framing gets wrong",
        "context": "If one of these three is a false choice, if there is a fourth OPEN item that should be on this page, or if you think the doc is arguing with itself somewhere — say so here. This is the field that gets read most carefully.",
        "field": {
          "kind": "textarea",
          "id": "framing-objection",
          "label": "What is missing, mis-stated, or not actually a decision?",
          "required": false
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
